In Java, Multiple inheritance is achieved through interfaces
Why ?
# Multiple Inheritance is nothing but inheriting 2 parents to single child (2 base class, 1 sub class)
# usually in multiple inheritance, above the base classes, object class will be there which acts as base class for other 2 classes 
# consider it as A class and B class is base class for C class and Object class is base class for A & B
# so when A and B inherits the properties of object class, then c class inherites the properties of A & B class but cannot inherit the properties of Object class
# this creates an ambiguity for C class without inherting the properties of Object class
# To avoid such cases, Java introduced Interfaces which does not inherit or connect with any object class
# so class C implements A, B (A & B are interfaces here) and C can inherit the properties of A & B without any ambiguity.

                             -------------------
                             |   class Object  |     (Ambiguity is caused because of Object class acts as base class for A & B)
                             -------------------
                                /           \
                              /               \
                    ----------------       -----------------
                    |    class A   |       |    class B    |
                    ----------------       -----------------
                            \                      /
